; Format-parameter lookup: one key table plus three parallel value tables,
; 18 entries each, $D419-$D460.
;
; Two bytes are read from the datastream via $E98C and combined at $D47E-$D48F:
;
;     p1 = byte1 & $0F        (also stored at $0488)
;     p2 = byte2 & $07        (also stored at $041E)
;     key = (p2 << 4) | p1
;
; $D492 then searches fmtKeys backwards from index $11, and the matching index
; selects one byte from each value table:
;
;     fmtVals041C -> $041C      $00/$FF flag
;     fmtVals041D -> $041D      $00/$FF flag
;     fmtVals041F -> $041F      $01 or $02
;
; The 18 keys are sorted and fall into three groups by p2 (1, 2 and 4), each
; holding the same six p1 values (6, 7, A, B, C, F).
;
; The search has no not-found path. If no key matches, DECB rolls 0 round to
; $FF, BPL fails, and execution falls into the value lookups with B = $FF,
; reading $D52A, $D53C and $D54E - 255 bytes past all three tables.
;
; That case IS reachable: $D40A gates entry on the first byte being $20, but the
; parameter bytes read afterwards at $D47E and $D483 are unconstrained, so p1
; spans 16 values and p2 spans 8 and 110 of the 128 possible keys miss.
;
; It is nevertheless harmless, because of what the three outputs feed:
;
;   $041C  gets $7F, and $D551 tests only bit 7 (BMI) - reads as clear, i.e.
;          the same as the intended $00
;   $041D  gets $F6, and $D5AA/$D6BB test only bit 7 (BPL) - reads as set, i.e.
;          the same as the intended $FF
;   $041F  gets $DD instead of 1 or 2, but its only consumer at $D706 does
;          ADDA $041F / CMPA #$7F / BCS / LDAA #$7F, so the result is clamped
;
; The three over-read addresses are ordinary ROM locations, so the access is
; well-defined on the bus. A missing bounds check with no crash or corruption
; consequence - worth knowing about, not worth calling a bug.
